This paper develops a conceptual framework for analysing bank resolution as a strategic interaction between resolution authorities and creditors. The framework models resolution as a signalling environment in which bailin performs a dual role: it allocates losses and conveys information about the credibility of the resolution authority. Creditor behaviour is therefore shaped not only by realised losses, but also by expectations regarding the effectiveness of resistance.&nbsp;
<div>
 The analysis shows that creditor resistance may arise as a rational response to resolution decisions rather than as evidence of flawed implementation. Higher losses increase incentives to resist, while higher credibility reduces the expected effectiveness of resistance. As a result, similar levels of bail-in may generate markedly different outcomes across institutional settings.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The framework further highlights a trade-off between creditor conflict and the feasibility of private-sector solutions. Extending the analysis beyond loss allocation, it shows how bail-in intensity may affect both creditor behaviour and the attractiveness of failing institutions to potential acquirers.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The framework is illustrated using selected European resolution cases, which exhibit patterns consistent with its main predictions. The analysis suggests that variation in resolution outcomes is closely linked to interactions between loss allocation, credibility, and creditor expectations. The paper contributes to the literature by introducing a strategic perspective on bank resolution, explicitly modelling creditors as strategic actors, and identifying credibility as a central determinant of resolution outcomes.
</div>

This research explores the implications for risk of six AI focused Fintech business models identified in the previous research. While each of the six business models has been validated in previous research their typical influence on risk has not been sufficiently explored.&nbsp;
<div>
 A Delphi method with two stages is used. In the first stage, the semi-structured interviews with experts identify the main risks each of the six models faces. For the second stage the participants are presented with a summary of all the risks identified by all the participants, and they are asked to rank them for their specific model.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Eight risks related to the application of an AI focused business model are identified: (1) direct AI risks, (2) technology integration risk, (3) third-party vendor risk, (4) technology infrastructure risk, (5) data protection and privacy risk, (6) cybersecurity risk, (7) fraud and identity theft risk, and (8) strategic misalignment risk. The eight risks apply to all six models however they are not equally significant in all models.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The six business models reduce risk in two main ways: Firstly, because they are proven models and secondly because they have a proven place in the typical ecosystems that emerge.
</div>
